# Kiro CLI. Model traffic and account state go to the hosts of the profile's region. Kiro offers two regions,
# us-east-1 and eu-central-1.
# - runtime serves inference.
# - management serves the profile and subscription state.
# The CLI tab of https://kiro.dev/docs/privacy-and-security/firewalls/ lists these hosts with their purposes.
# Only us-east-1 was measured; eu-central-1 is inspected on the same client's evidence.
allow https://runtime.us-east-1.kiro.dev/       read method=POST,PUT,PATCH,DELETE
allow https://management.us-east-1.kiro.dev/    read method=POST,PUT,PATCH,DELETE
allow https://runtime.eu-central-1.kiro.dev/    read method=POST,PUT,PATCH,DELETE
allow https://management.eu-central-1.kiro.dev/ read method=POST,PUT,PATCH,DELETE

# The same page lists the legacy q.<region>.amazonaws.com as required. Only the V2 engine, kiro-cli's own `acp` child,
# sends its model calls there; V3 sent it nothing. Kiro's startup prompt says that V2 "will be removed in January",
# and V2 is the default only for a headless run without --trust-all-tools. So the host has no line, and the proxy's
# refusal names it. A V2 run needs --v3 in its place, or the workspace's own
# `allow https://q.us-east-1.amazonaws.com/ read method=POST,PUT,PATCH,DELETE` line, under which V2's turns completed.

# Sign-in, `kiro-cli login --use-device-flow`: the CLI prints the code, and the browser approves the code. The CLI
# then polls for the tokens and refreshes them at a host that depends on the account kind.
# - Google and GitHub accounts use Kiro's own host. The page also lists Cognito federation for them, which the Google
#   sign-in did not call, so cognito-identity.us-east-1.amazonaws.com has no line.
# - Builder ID and IAM Identity Center use AWS SSO OIDC in the instance's region. Builder ID's region is us-east-1. It
#   is inspected on the evidence of the same client's AWS SDK at management.
# - An Identity Center in another region needs a workspace's own
#   `allow https://oidc.<region>.amazonaws.com/ read method=POST,PUT,PATCH,DELETE` line. Without the line, the sign-in
#   fails with `error: dispatch failure`, and the proxy log names the refused host.
allow https://prod.us-east-1.auth.desktop.kiro.dev/ read method=POST,PUT,PATCH,DELETE
allow https://oidc.us-east-1.amazonaws.com/         read method=POST,PUT,PATCH,DELETE

# These hosts of the same list are absent, and Kiro works without them:
# - telemetry.<region>.kiro.dev, client-telemetry.<region>.amazonaws.com and prod.us-east-1.telemetry.desktop.kiro.dev
#   receive telemetry. Each attempt leaves a refusal in the log, or none with `kiro-cli settings telemetry.enabled
#   false`.
# - app.kiro.dev serves the device sign-in page, which the browser on the host opens. V3 also reads its user settings
#   and a cloud configuration there at each start.
# - prod.download.cli.kiro.dev and desktop-release.q.us-east-1.amazonaws.com serve updates and installer downloads,
#   which the image turns off (app.disableAutoupdates). V2 also downloads its semantic-search model from
#   desktop-release.
# - cli.kiro.dev serves the install script.

# Measured with kiro-cli 3.3.0 on 2026-11-14, signed in with a Google account in us-east-1, under deny-unless-allowed
# (doc/plan-tls-inspect-agents.md, "Measurement"):
# - Modes: kiro-cli chat --v3 --no-interactive with a shell call and a subagent that makes its own shell call;
#   --resume-id with a shell call; the V3 TUI in this repository, with two turns, /compact and a message; kiro-cli
#   logout, kiro-cli login --use-device-flow and a turn; a token refresh at the token's expiry; the TUI with a turn
#   under deny-unless-model.
# - The path, refused and error lines are what MeasurementReport accepts (doc/plan-tls-inspect-agents.md, "The
#   measurement suite").
#   path runtime.us-east-1.kiro.dev / /v1/responses
#   path management.us-east-1.kiro.dev /
#   path prod.us-east-1.auth.desktop.kiro.dev /oauth/device/authorization /oauth/device/poll /logout /refreshToken
#   refused telemetry.us-east-1.kiro.dev CONNECT
#   refused client-telemetry.us-east-1.amazonaws.com CONNECT
#   refused app.kiro.dev CONNECT
#   refused prod.download.cli.kiro.dev CONNECT
#   refused cognito-identity.us-east-1.amazonaws.com CONNECT
#   error management.us-east-1.kiro.dev tunnel: expected 5 bytes, got 0
# - Each mode ran in a Node child of kiro-cli, which sent the model calls as KiroCLI/3.3.0 and the rest as
#   aws-sdk-js. kiro-cli itself sent the sign-in requests as Kiro-CLI, and read the profile at management as
#   aws-sdk-rust. Every client negotiated HTTP/1.1 and trusted the launch CA.
# - Within each sign-in, every request to runtime and management carried the same credential index. The sign-in
#   host's codes and tokens travel in bodies, so its lines carry no cred=.
# - Every body declared a Content-Length; /v1/responses bodies held 54 to 63 KB. No query held a credential.
# - A request to / names its operation in a header, as AWS's JSON protocol does, so its line does not.
# - management's query names the Kiro profile's ARN, which holds an AWS account number.
# - The sign-in polled /oauth/device/poll every five seconds until the browser approved the code. The logout sent
#   /logout.
# - The refusals: telemetry every few seconds while Kiro runs; app.kiro.dev twice at each start; the update host at the
#   TUI's start.
# - The error: once, at a V3 start, a CONNECT to management sent no byte before closing, and the turn completed.
# - Signed out, kiro-cli whoami sent cognito-identity two requests without a credential. With the host refused,
#   kiro-cli tried it three to six times at each start, and the turns completed.
# - At the token's expiry, kiro-cli refreshed the token at /refreshToken, and the turn completed. A refresh forced by
#   moving the stored expiry 50 minutes early was answered 401 there instead, and kiro-cli deleted the token.
# - A token from a sign-in three minutes after that 401 was rejected from its first use: "The bearer token included in
#   the request is invalid", in Kiro's log, under both profiles. The next sign-in's token worked and refreshed.
# - Unmeasured: eu-central-1; the Builder ID and Identity Center sign-ins at oidc; the GitHub sign-in.
